Iliotibial Band (ITB) Syndrome
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Lateral knee pain related to cyclical motion, Focal tenderness over lateral femoral epicondyle, Pain maximal at 30 degrees knee flexion

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-027
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe lateral knee pain causing sudden inability to walk, particularly on stairs, following a prolonged period of unaccustomed physical exertion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage determined that a previously healthy patient developed acute, sharp, functionally limiting knee pain after a long-distance run, rendering them completely unable to navigate the stairs in their home. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to differentiate acute ITB friction syndrome from a lateral meniscus tear or lateral collateral ligament sprain, and to provide immediate mechanical offloading and analgesia to allow basic mobility prior to regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My knee was fine for the first 10 kilometers, but then a sharp, burning stabbing started on the outside; now I can't even bend it to walk down the stairs."
History of Present Illness: Patient (a novice runner training for a half-marathon) presents with an acute onset of severe right lateral knee pain. Timeline: Mild tightness noted during the last 3 training runs; today, at precisely the 10km mark, a sharp, burning pain localized to the lateral aspect of the right knee emerged. Patient attempted to push through, but the pain became excruciating, forcing them to stop. Currently reports that any attempt to walk, specifically descending stairs or walking downhill, significantly intensifies the lateral knee discomfort. Describes a sensation of snapping or intense friction across the lateral knee during movement without any true intra-articular locking or giving way.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a stiff-legged, peg-leg gait to avoid knee flexion; completely unable to negotiate stairs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Bedroom and bathroom located on the second floor; patient is stranded on the ground level.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, frustrated, resting with the right leg completely straight.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals no visible swelling, erythema, or gross deformity of the right knee. Crucially, there is a distinct absence of intra-articular joint effusion or deep joint line tenderness characteristic of meniscal pathology. Focal, severe tenderness palpated directly over the lateral femoral epicondyle, approximately 2-3 cm proximal to the joint line. Positive Noble compression test: applying firm thumb pressure over the lateral epicondyle as the knee is passively extended from 90 degrees produces a sharp, reproducible pain exactly at 30 degrees of flexion. Positive Ober's test demonstrating severe restricted flexibility and tightness of the iliotibial band. Ligamentous testing (ACL, PCL, MCL, LCL) is stable and painless.
Environmental Observations: Two flights of stairs separate the patient from their sleeping quarters.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Applied ice 1 hour ago with transient, mild relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Iliotibial Band (ITB) Friction Syndrome - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Lateral Meniscus Tear - Ruled out clinically by the absence of a twisting injury mechanism, negative McMurray's test, lack of deep joint line tenderness, and absence of an intra-articular effusion.

Lateral Collateral Ligament (LCL) Sprain - Ruled out by a stable and painless varus stress test at 0 and 30 degrees of flexion.

Patellofemoral Pain Syndrome - Ruled out by negative patellar grind test and pain specifically localized to the lateral epicondyle rather than diffusely anterior or retropatellar.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a classic, severe, acute-onset ITB friction syndrome triggered by cyclical overload. The history of a specific distance onset and the localized burning pain, combined with a positive Noble compression test, definitively localized the pathology to the extra-articular lateral femoral epicondyle. The functional impairment was significant, rendering the patient unable to bend the knee or safely navigate the stairs in their home. Postponing medical evaluation would have left the patient stranded on the ground floor with unmanaged, sharp mechanical pain. Urgent after-hours assessment confidently excluded internal derangement (meniscus/ligament tear), preventing an unnecessary ED visit for MRI, and permitted the immediate initiation of conservative anti-inflammatory management and functional gait modification.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Ibuprofen 400mg TDS with food for 5-7 days. Applied a topical NSAID gel (Diclofenac) specifically to the lateral epicondyle.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Use a firm foam roller exclusively on the thickest part of the upper thigh (tensor fasciae latae muscle belly), explicitly avoiding rolling directly over the painful bony bump at the knee. Consequence: Release of the tight proximal muscle pulling on the IT band, reducing the downstream friction at the knee and immediately decreasing the sharp, snapping pain (positive reinforcement).
Action: Descend the stairs strictly using the "good leg down, bad leg straight" method for the next 48 hours. Consequence: Prevention of the knee bending through the painful 30-degree friction arc while under bodyweight, completely avoiding the agonizing stabbing sensation (negative reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory referral to a sports physiotherapist for gait analysis, gluteal strengthening, and structured return-to-run program.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1-2 weeks. Absolute cessation of running until pain-free with daily activities.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the knee rapidly balloons with fluid, becomes hot and red, or if the joint completely locks in a bent position and cannot be straightened.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, based on functional testing and history. MRI is only required if symptoms are refractory to prolonged conservative management. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute, burning inflammation will subside with rest and NSAIDs over 5-10 days. However, returning to running without altering running biomechanics and strengthening the hip stabilizers will guarantee an immediate recurrence.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your knee suddenly swells up massively, becomes hot to the touch, or locks entirely so you cannot straighten your leg.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The thick band of tissue on the outside of your leg is excessively tight and is violently snapping back and forth over the bony bump on the side of your knee with every step. The resulting friction has caused a severe localized burn-like inflammation. The anti-inflammatories will calm the chemical fire, but only strict rest and eventual physiotherapy will fix the underlying tightness.
Actionable Steps: Do not bend your knee under weight. Go down the stairs backward or with a straight, stiff leg to avoid hitting that painful 30-degree angle. Use ice directly on the side of the knee for 15 minutes, 4 times a day.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Iliotibial Band (ITB) Syndrome (severe friction inflammation). Rest, ice the outside of the knee, and take anti-inflammatories. Do not run. See sports physio ASAP.

SMS EDUCATION

When going down stairs, step down with your good leg first, keeping your bad leg completely straight. This stops the tight band from snapping over the knee bone and causing sharp pain.
